#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "c262f28d-bfd0-5c78-bce7-d2ae314052e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5510" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/b2240a11f1760050914a9c02554e0b7e86cd84d420fb437a43d95b474103b26c.png) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}+A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}+A-2I) & =O \\\\ A+I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $q=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "8e6d3017-d05d-5a2d-8043-c827e612476e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5511" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18.$ If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y,$ then find the value of $x+y.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-4A+18I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y = -4+18 = 14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "ce724b3b-f440-5c1f-86a6-c50a54931233" :question/math-academy-id "e-5512" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/308850190e8483ae48e8179585c01f1805e6caf92e4d4254c996bd0ffa800427.png) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124279 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "95b44b17-b796-51eb-93dd-4ee4b2e5c3f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5894" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/cb48b48ef82e5d6cfe903488e80a8cbd48e286c864ba387450e01430e718d77c.png) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "990ef91e-eca3-5777-91c0-2c5ae65c294e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A+4I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335699} {:answer/id #uuid "81e3eabc-207d-5fc9-944f-bb1ded84029b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335700} {:answer/id #uuid "cfdac069-0360-5bf1-b262-0e5546406782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335701} {:answer/id #uuid "6769f0be-8143-5054-a64d-acf94b508ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A-2I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335702} {:answer/id #uuid "ff120a2c-e1a3-5bd2-afe8-638f481fc9c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff120a2c-e1a3-5bd2-afe8-638f481fc9c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335703} :answer-field/id #uuid "efcec902-c1b7-5cbf-90de-9739e977f0fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d3ee196-f513-5f13-b73d-fe743c26ad51" :question/math-academy-id "q-140521" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=3$ and $\\text{det}(A)=-7.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =3A-(-7)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =3A+7I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150a5032-f81a-5eaa-bc1e-e1352b9f3845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A-7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335706} {:answer/id #uuid "aa523aeb-cb7d-5aab-9918-0913fd312d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2A-7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335707} {:answer/id #uuid "17d58f75-2782-575e-937b-0af1a25a1e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5A-7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335708} {:answer/id #uuid "b2efc790-02d5-5ba0-b2e5-8e52fc279e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5A-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335709} {:answer/id #uuid "9541f9d9-e203-5219-abb8-452dabc85abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5A-6I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d58f75-2782-575e-937b-0af1a25a1e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5A-7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335708} :answer-field/id #uuid "14190b8e-827e-53be-818c-2658c8dc3c82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d91675b6-3079-5369-b54d-5b4e498138a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-140522" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=-{\\lambda }^{3}+5\\lambda -7.$ Then, ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{3}+5A-7I & =O \\\\ {A}^{3} & =5A-7I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a26715-cfc2-5601-af13-80bf22ec0943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335713} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8f8ff8-1fc7-5703-93e5-8fbed535e604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -42 \\\\ 14 & -31\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335714} {:answer/id #uuid "e62e7d44-5f44-56a2-9946-9f00457595bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 12 \\\\ -24 & -31\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335715} {:answer/id #uuid "1b39e8f6-0474-5c6f-b312-0ef2bee294ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}31 & 14 \\\\ 42 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335716} {:answer/id #uuid "f307dc9e-141f-5c15-8531-f742680d06a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 14 \\\\ -42 & 30\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15a26715-cfc2-5601-af13-80bf22ec0943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335713} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d1fd8c4-4982-5b73-bc7e-d1a4c49688bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90d489f4-f1b4-5826-a3b4-cd8aff951e6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140602" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{16}-4{x}^{14}+7x-3\\,$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+12 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{14}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{16}\\,-\\,4{x}^{14}\\,+\\,7x\\,-\\,3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{16}$ | $-$ | $4{x}^{14}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $7x$ | $-$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{14}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =7A-3I \\\\  & =7[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -28\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6aedb12-1068-53e1-8269-98dbfe89eae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4A-9I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335720} {:answer/id #uuid "459b5c44-2ee4-55e4-a502-5666761117c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40A+39I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335721} {:answer/id #uuid "f6760922-3944-5928-9a03-17f001a25f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40A-39I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335722} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6a5281-f277-53c2-9771-6ad98efbeded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A-3I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335723} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c1fd2d-59bc-5ce7-911e-4e9c37cf9c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-39A-40I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6760922-3944-5928-9a03-17f001a25f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40A-39I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335722} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa31a76-c08d-58b8-a2cc-9c76549a657b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "777c12b6-f542-568b-b040-3cd16301d6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37777" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+4x+3,$ then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+4x+3,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $4x$ | $+$ | $13$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+4x+3\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $+$ | $3{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $3{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $16{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $12x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $13{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $12x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $13{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $52x$ | $+$ | $39$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $-$ | $40x$ | $-$ | $39$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-40x-39),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-4x+13.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =-40A-39I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db022e60-b4a3-5fed-a684-66f4384b0da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123101} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd446df-b6d7-5613-be91-a05c4299f3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123102} {:answer/id #uuid "4a97d7f1-0d82-5c27-a742-610ee8832586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123103} {:answer/id #uuid "68aa5ebf-5e46-560a-9bcb-e8c435dcc742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123104} {:answer/id #uuid "70868969-3b44-542c-836c-06a1bc12bd70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70868969-3b44-542c-836c-06a1bc12bd70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123105} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc50ce1-88c5-57fe-b2fa-149c8b0f42fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4acccd18-c797-5d41-ab58-67f98662f9b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37779" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+9I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+9I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+9{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{8}{9}I-\\frac{1}{9}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{9},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{9},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{8}{9}+(-\\frac{1}{9})=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9240c62-1467-5833-b3d1-0d888c003822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{4}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335727} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3d890a-ac9a-51e8-b2cf-20e316b06fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335728} {:answer/id #uuid "8aeac5df-3477-5833-bd74-77b4b093bac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{1}{5}A+\\frac{2}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335729} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3bf075-052d-564f-a254-b01064ab26c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5I-4A-5{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335730} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb5465e-a77c-5890-93ee-60d0a6082d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2I+4A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d3d890a-ac9a-51e8-b2cf-20e316b06fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335728} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b2e733-2ac0-5ba5-9637-8154cf7d6a7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "544f8b26-26dd-59f6-ae3d-e40288df33a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37831" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+1) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+2A-4I-5{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca7be6c6-77ce-5830-a27b-e4e87375e74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335734} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0d3e9b-c681-55da-8838-335af666adc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735} {:answer/id #uuid "a36b5ef8-f1ee-5a49-9b22-a8fd509dbce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335736} {:answer/id #uuid "47f091bc-9f0a-5a78-81b9-2cbcfeaf6b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335737} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e839bf-4ae5-50b5-bdc4-f1d49eb6db2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa0d3e9b-c681-55da-8838-335af666adc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffdc1c38-f4aa-53b1-baca-a1de7440c70b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f265b9ef-7603-5f92-bdb0-6bd466019f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-38703" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da91b46b-efcd-5c12-a89b-c90af6668819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335741} {:answer/id #uuid "03ffd024-3fb4-5b30-a0be-7824b03dde4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335742} {:answer/id #uuid "4e291f18-ed0a-50d2-8e47-0cc2d9eb702b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335743} {:answer/id #uuid "10b1581d-57da-51e2-8979-53c8b6e6d009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335744} {:answer/id #uuid "1d25ed09-6685-58c6-a606-6741b910d978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b1581d-57da-51e2-8979-53c8b6e6d009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335744} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6f0651-da6c-5306-be03-896b713afebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ef5253a-11f4-5a9b-9e83-e789d7c37cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-38704" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+6I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+6I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+6{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 6{A}^{-1} & =8I-A \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{4}{3}I-\\frac{1}{6}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{4}{3}+(-\\frac{1}{6})=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e73cb5-bc0b-5389-b003-a61a4c7b183b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 40 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335748} {:answer/id #uuid "0eae06e8-3875-55e3-9464-04b45cfa385b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 22 \\\\ -42 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335749} {:answer/id #uuid "efdf39f1-f416-55ad-b716-51859093a27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335750} {:answer/id #uuid "bdafffea-1fd3-5a79-b473-7e775e8199a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 40 \\\\ 0 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335751} {:answer/id #uuid "ca60aeea-7d81-5a84-98a9-db0848834037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca60aeea-7d81-5a84-98a9-db0848834037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335752} :answer-field/id #uuid "a39fb50b-962b-52ba-9b84-9510fca09033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6884fbdf-c141-559a-9c69-73ca1eb1083a" :question/math-academy-id "q-38727" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{23}-25{x}^{21}-4x+2$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{21}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-25\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{23}\\,-\\,25{x}^{21}\\,-\\,4x\\,+\\,2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{23}$ | $-$ | $25{x}^{21}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  | $-$ | $4x$ | $+$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(-4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{21}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(-4A+2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(-4A+2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-4A+2I) \\\\  & =-4A+2I \\\\  & =-4[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5096ca51-419c-5522-9153-7890e3873a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335755} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7eab16-d734-545c-af43-bc3458f5b263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335756} {:answer/id #uuid "fc194bfd-b97d-5e8d-b5c1-526a46e615b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335757} {:answer/id #uuid "6541be73-2bf8-5064-b2ee-f2c7ff192d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335758} {:answer/id #uuid "761b76f8-418d-50e2-8bdf-3a682b7dd59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "761b76f8-418d-50e2-8bdf-3a682b7dd59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335759} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40dd4a5-8cbd-5323-be99-2a5ad79c32a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b1afa91-6737-55ce-9772-a6317b97a694" :question/math-academy-id "q-40173" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-5A-2 & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-5A-2) & ={A}^{-1}O \\\\ A-5I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{5}{2}I+\\frac{1}{2}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{5}{2},$ $q=\\frac{1}{2}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a1d694-e70d-56ed-b78a-b789f8132c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335762} {:answer/id #uuid "f17fe68c-8657-591e-bc9f-c04656c4e021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335763} {:answer/id #uuid "365136d3-a893-5371-9c59-1ec7a6d53123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335764} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b50d2d-2448-5991-a201-2ebd924ef2df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335765} {:answer/id #uuid "f9997526-49ca-5017-b9bc-9755f38d6254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "365136d3-a893-5371-9c59-1ec7a6d53123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335764} :answer-field/id #uuid "59edf087-2176-5615-a039-d0996d711afe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1246d12-9869-5048-ac94-75758eae4480" :question/math-academy-id "q-83390" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=5-6=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66b9e81c-3b1b-5f10-a4ea-ea51e6b81955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335769} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c55fc9-bf2f-59c5-80ef-e6a1376d72a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335770} {:answer/id #uuid "9793f2c2-25cd-52c8-b604-a6c89bd9f06b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335771} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0974f7-7142-597d-80c3-ce37536cbffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335772} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfe748c-cea8-5c05-b8b7-b4fb9d3ee9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cfe748c-cea8-5c05-b8b7-b4fb9d3ee9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335773} :answer-field/id #uuid "d82d699d-6321-57ca-9708-940daaed09ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ea0af53-f2fd-5a94-9857-a538772380ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-83391" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 7-(-5)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-28+15 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74263b8a-e843-5b1e-9f7d-ba1add9ae7f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335776} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ab2057-27d7-556d-95c1-15c27ec91175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335777} {:answer/id #uuid "7b32cda2-edd7-5d90-bae0-f8834606abd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335778} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0f81f4-d420-5154-9b57-a836739903f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335779} {:answer/id #uuid "28e9b74f-4942-588d-8846-8ec8f80d2247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ab2057-27d7-556d-95c1-15c27ec91175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335777} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5f2fe2-d4c8-5427-9ee6-71a6b2494263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cebfd11-81a6-5d58-a074-6a39a1866435" :question/math-academy-id "q-83393" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =10-24 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c327c4a-e6c3-5406-87de-ac028fbf7d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7A-17I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a7b3bc-90a7-5a0a-a700-0da7c401873f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/ma