e determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 2-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =-7\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7aa005-10c2-590c-bd2d-40ab6554ef7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2A+22I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335804} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ec4425-f31a-5c2f-ad84-e933df732800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2A-22I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335805} {:answer/id #uuid "fff7ee05-40c9-5b9a-be2c-4e2890b604ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{A}^{2}-22I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335806} {:answer/id #uuid "6bfeb839-dc29-5265-81f2-582310fbe8cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{A}^{2}+22I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335807} {:answer/id #uuid "b87dce20-04c7-552c-935c-55dd066e0a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22{A}^{2}+2I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bfeb839-dc29-5265-81f2-582310fbe8cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{A}^{2}+22I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335807} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a49c063-a51b-5141-b653-9d4e82e0c6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b50be690-fd08-5683-b002-050ce8968ba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85926" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=-{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}+22.$ Then, ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}+22I & =O \\\\ {A}^{3} & =2{A}^{2}+22I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f6e2018-a1ae-5db5-a4fb-933dd759cec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335811} {:answer/id #uuid "368c154c-f801-5c3e-bfc2-3102afebe4d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335812} {:answer/id #uuid "add64616-c9db-5960-bad9-158a2ab4ae8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49I-12A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335813} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0ed08f-af9f-5110-84c6-6368a02b6622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335814} {:answer/id #uuid "585af983-247e-541c-a8cd-8617a9ffd49f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I+2A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "368c154c-f801-5c3e-bfc2-3102afebe4d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335812} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7a7ec0-7db0-5fc6-967d-3f7b1d9e642a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba3fffe7-2f8e-5856-806c-9ddcf2cb6da2" :question/math-academy-id "q-85929" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +29) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+12{\\lambda }^{2}-49\\lambda +58 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-49A+58I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}(-{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-49A+58I) & =O \\\\ -{A}^{2}+12A-49I+58{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{58}(49I-12A+{A}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f7e3c5-0846-5f35-879e-4681639df46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20A-21I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335818} {:answer/id #uuid "8f867db1-a6f5-5020-867d-9f0aeda37c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21A-20I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335819} {:answer/id #uuid "81c7bace-cf4a-5350-83ed-c27e6841cbbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21A+20I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335820} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb4b441-2dea-5cb5-83b3-21a1ad8bcfaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21A+20I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335821} {:answer/id #uuid "758f02ed-0adc-542a-88d6-11185dbbad8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20A+21I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb4b441-2dea-5cb5-83b3-21a1ad8bcfaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21A+20I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335821} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0deb7fd-7d95-5792-a182-81d13a7b3ce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "629a4e6c-f5bf-5ddb-918c-4df8c28eff1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85931" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $+$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-x-20\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $20x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $20x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $x$ | $-$ | $20$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $21x$ | $+$ | $20$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(21x+20),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x+1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =21A+20I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "675a9482-82be-54c9-b3dd-5b826e29f225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 0 \\\\ 72 & -27\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335825} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cfb567-7a5f-5795-a799-194d6557d732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}27 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335826} {:answer/id #uuid "25ff0b48-1b15-5ed7-a36a-d036de14995d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335827} {:answer/id #uuid "5484591e-6e12-55fb-82b7-ca236204781f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335828} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cd1d8a-a720-569a-ab63-08d1c6ecc922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 27 \\\\ 0 & -72\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ff0b48-1b15-5ed7-a36a-d036de14995d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335827} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3638314-7918-5275-beb0-23fe0c31e1b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ce7302d-756f-51f6-96fc-8dd63d5b081e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85993" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{21}-16{x}^{19}+6x-3$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{19}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-16\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{21}\\,-\\,16{x}^{19}\\,+\\,6x\\,\\,-\\,\\,3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{21}$ | $-$ | $16{x}^{19}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $6x$ | $-$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(6x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{19}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =6A-3I \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
